# https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Atom> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "standalone atom"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An atom that does not share electrons with other atoms."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StandaloneAtom> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "StandaloneAtom"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#ContinuumSubstance> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "state of matter"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A superclass made as the disjoint union of all the form under which matter can exist."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "StateOfMatter"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#StateOfMatter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "In physics, a state of matter is one of the distinct forms in which matter can exist. Four states of matter are observable in everyday life: solid, liquid, gas, and plasma."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "substance"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A matter entity characterised by the fact that it possesses some homogeneous properties or identification criteria."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A litre of water."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A pen can be classified as a substance (although it has parts with different properties) since it can be identified as being solid."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Substance"@en .
# 
